so we've talked a lot on the show about  the scaling up of the trade war with  China that Donald Trump has waged which  has actually increased our trade deficit  with them even though Donald Trump said  he was going to get rid of that trade  deficit and end up with a trade surplus  with China hasn't happened the wacky  tariffs have actually made it even worse  so that's China and we've talked about  that but today I want to talk about the  trade war with Europe at least to get us  into a more general conversation about  tariffs and this is a bigger fight than  just related to Donald Trump this is  part of the Airbus versus Boeing fight  that exists Boeing of course the  us-based airline airplane manufacturer  and Airbus is a European aircraft  manufacturer and Donald Trump is  complaining about European subsidies on  Airbus recently tweeting quote the World  Trade Organization finds that the  European Union subsidies to Airbus has  adversely impacted the United States  which will now put tariffs on 11 billion  dollars of EU products the EU has taken  advantage of the US on trade for many  years it will soon stop so what Donald  Trump is going to do is find 11 billion  dollars in European goods to slap some  tariffs on - and the problem of course  like with many of these tariff ideas  that Donald Trump has and sometimes puts  into into action the problem is that  it's not going to solve the Boeing  Airbus issue which supposedly is what  Donald Trump is concerned with it's just  going to make Americans have to pay more  for European stuff and the other sort of  weird thing about this is that the World  Trade Organization already ruled that  Europe subsidies on Airbus were illegal  back in May of 2018 and at the same time  the United States also subsidizes Boeing  and Europe is preparing a list of  American products to put tariffs on - to  punish us for our subsidies on Boeing  and you can very quickly see how this is  an escalation of tension with our  supposed allies in a way that's not  going to achieve what Donald Trump  claims he wants to achieve on trade and  that's a really important thing when we  analyze the effect of these polit  policies and tariffs we can analyze them  generally for the record  compact but we also have to compare them  with Donald Trump stated goals for  putting them in and one of those goals  with China for example was to reduce the  trade deficit and in fact his policy  with China on trade and tariffs has done  the opposite we have a record-high trade  deficit with China and what we're doing  in practice is sort of retaliating  against admittedly bad economics with  more bad economics and Trump repeats  this over and over again while claiming  that he's the only guy who can fix it  and if you have like even the most basic  understanding of basic economics and  globalization you would then be in a  position to realize why this is the  political equivalent of a penis size  contest but where you're not even really  sure what the penis is like you've got  someone comparing the size of their  penis to someone else's thumb right like  I it's kind of a cartoonish analogy but  it's you take a penis size contest which  in and of itself is not a particularly  useful thing and then you realize  someone is not even actually looking at  the size of their penis in the  comparison so to speak if people knew  more about economics and trade they  might never have fallen for Trump's  bogus promises during the campaign to  begin with about how he's gonna fix  NAFTA and he's going to fix China and  all of this stuff but his base fell very  hard for it and now Trump is making the  problems even worse and there are some  reasonable arguments to be made for  tariffs in certain situations but there  are some fundamental truths about how  tariffs work that are completely missing  here number one trade wars aren't easy  to win as Donald Trump likes to claim  they're easy to start they're easy to  escalate they're easy to get completely  stuck in and extend but there's a point  that's missing which is that when you  put tariffs on European or Chinese goods  it's really a tax on Americans and what  I mean by that is that when you tariff a  foreign good you're effectively adding a  tax on Americans whether it's American  individuals or companies when they buy  that foreign good so it doesn't bring  jobs back to the United States it  doesn't create jobs it doesn't decrease  the trade deficit as Donnell  Trump promised it makes it grow actually  and at a larger level these trade wars  create uncertainty which is rarely good  for world markets it's rarely good for  domestic markets and in particular if  you see a world economic power like the  United States and you see them as a  potentially bad partner or as an  unreliable partner in trade it's not  good and that is sort of the the epitome  of what we are seeing here in Trump's  uninformed trade policy and most  disturbingly he continues to have his  base tricked into believing he  understands this stuff and he's doing  the right thing and it is imploding in  front of our very eyes every single week  and this 11 billion is just going to  make it even worse
 

